  {
    "episode_id": 72696,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72696.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Yes. And yes, I'll start with the 1.2 trillion dollars over over eight years. because I was one of the ones that helped negotiate that package. We should have language out on it that will dot the eyes and cross the tees and tell folks how this is going to be implemented. It will be taken up first and then to let your listeners know that that package deals with infrastructure like roads and bridges and electrical grid, broadband, water infrastructure, those kind of things, traditional infrastructure. It's a significant investment. It's $579 billion over baseline. They get to 1.3 by using baseline dollars that are traditionally allocated anyway, by the way. But it's the biggest investment infrastructure ever. And it's something that we need and it's something that I'm glad to have been a part of. There's another package, a $3.5 trillion package to that. was put out by the Budget Committee that was just released. And in fact, I don't know near as much about the specifics because the information hasn't been released yet. I do know that it's going to deal in part with things like housing, childcare, VA facilities, senior care. Those are four things that I'm reasonably sure are going to be in it, but there hasn't been a lot of eyes dotted at East Cross about how that's going to happen. Now, I'll be honest with you. I think we need investments in both. We need investments from both traditional infrastructure. When I go around the state of Montana, as I did over the 4th of July, everywhere I stopped, I heard about the lack of work for housing. I heard about the fact that childcare costs so much that people can't afford to go to work And then I heard about the need for more workers for workforce training. So we will see what happens as with anything, you know, where the rubber meets the road is where I'm going to make my decision. I think we need to have a debate on the 3.5 trillion. And if we're able to, you know, make some inroads into these areas of housing and VA facilities and child care, Um, it, you know, it's, it would be a step in the right direction, but we'll see how that's done. So we're early, we're early on the 3.5 one. I'll have more information for you and, and over the next month on it.",
