    "turn_text": "the meantime, we're working with Secretary Vilsack. He announced yesterday he's got about 500 billion dollars that they're going to put out there for loans, at least a portion of that for loans, for startup and expanding existing meat processing. What will that do? That'll let a small meat processing plant in Montana or any other state be able to expand and address and be competitive with the big boys. And that'll put more competition in the marketplace. And I think it'll end up with a fairer market. and more competition. I've also got some stuff that I'm doing all the stuff with Drassley. He's been a leader on this and has a buy on these issues for him longer than I have because he's been here a lot longer than I am. But we've also got another one that sets a cash price of cattle at 50%. Right now it's 10% or 12%. And when it's that low, those cash prices can be manipulated to deal with the formula pricing and drive those prices down. Bottom line is this. We've got a problem and we need to address it. It's a problem that has been around for a long time. I think we're starting to see people on both sides of the aisle take notice of this. So if we can get a bipartisan push to get some of these things done, I think it can make a big difference to the bottom line of our cow-calf producers and our small and medium-sized feeders be allowed to stay in business and make an honest living. and have a fair market. And that's really where it's at. And we've got to fix this. If we don't fix this and we don't fix it soon, I'm going to tell you, our food security is not going to be where we need it to be in this country. And I'm passionate about this because I've seen many of my friends and neighbors go broke, not because they're bad operators, because they were in a system that was rigged for the Packers. And we need to make sure that that rig in stops.",
