  {
    "episode_id": 72696,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72696.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "You're very astute. Yes, indeed. You're exactly correct. We've got to have the process. We've got to have the inspectors out there. And then we've got to allow for, I believe, you know, it's the same standard where there's state or federal inspections. This is all safe food, the safest food in the world, best food in the world, too. Allow these folks to be able to sell across state lines. Let me take Montana, for example. We got more cows than we got people. We got just over a million people in our state. If you allow a packing plant, a small packing plant to be able to sell across state lines, that gives them access to Spokane, Washington, Seattle, Washington, the Salt Lake City, Denver, Minneapolis, Fargo. I mean, it's just all around us. There's opportunities there to be able to employ people, process, meet without shipping them halfway around the world and back again. And so it's just a win-win deal all the way around. And by the way, I would just say that it would just connect the consumer up a lot closer with the producer. And I think that's always healthy. If you can keep food closer to where it's being raised, being processed and sold. I think that's a win-win deal.",
