  {
    "episode_id": 72696,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72696.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Look, that's a concern by some and I can tell you that if what we had going on right now worked, I wouldn't be talking to you about it. If there was competition in the marketplace and we had folks, you know, if it was a fair marketplace, I would say, stay the hell out federal government. But right now, it ain't working. And so we need to put some sideboards on it. I think we always have to be cognizant of the fact that we don't want, you know, we don't want big brother in there and wants the problems fixed. I think this problem can be fixed with just enforcement of a bill that was passed 100 over 100 years ago, the Packers and Stockyards Act. I don't think it's a heavy hand to government. I think it's just ensuring that we have competition in the marketplace. And I will tell you that I think that's fundamental to capitalism and it's fundamental to our democracy. And so once that's enforced and they know that there's folks that are ensuring that farmers and ranchers are getting a fair price, I think we're off and we're going. But no, I'm not as worried about that as I am about letting the situation that we have now continue. And then I guarantee we're going to have our food processing. Food for the consumers, the food chain is going to be severely altered.",
